## 2. Deconstructing the Memory Footprint of an LLM

To optimize memory usage, we must first mathematically break down where GPU VRAM is consumed during runtime:

$$\text{Total VRAM Required} = M_{\text{weights}} + M_{\text{KV}} + M_{\text{activations}} + M_{\text{system}}$$

```
┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
│                         GPU VRAM Allocation Breakdown                       │
├──────────────────────────────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────┤
│  Static Allocation (Fixed)           │  Dynamic Allocation (Scales with S)  │
├──────────────────────────────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────┤
│  • Model Weights (M_weights)         │  • KV Cache (M_KV = 2·B·L·H_kv·d_k·S)│
│    FP16: ~2 GB per billion params    │    Grows continuously per token!     │
│    FP8:  ~1 GB per billion params    │  • Ephemeral Activations (M_act)     │
│    INT4: ~0.5 GB per billion params  │  • Temp Workspace Buffers (Scratch)  │
│  • CUDA Runtime / Driver Context     │                                      │
└──────────────────────────────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────┘
```

### 1. Model Weights ($M_{\text{weights}}$)
The static baseline memory required to hold the model parameters in accelerator memory:
$$M_{\text{weights}} = N_{\text{params}} \times P_{\text{bytes}}$$
* **FP16 / BF16 ($P_{\text{bytes}} = 2$)**: A 70B parameter model requires $70 \times 10^9 \times 2 \approx \mathbf{140 \text{ GB}}$ of static VRAM.
* **FP8 ($P_{\text{bytes}} = 1$)**: Reduces the static footprint to $\approx \mathbf{70 \text{ GB}}$.
* **INT4 ($P_{\text{bytes}} = 0.5$)**: Reduces the static footprint to $\approx \mathbf{35 \text{ GB}}$.

### 2. Key-Value Cache ($M_{\text{KV}}$)
The dynamic, sequence-dependent memory holding the Key and Value activation vectors for all past tokens across all layers:
$$M_{\text{KV}} = 2 \times B \times n_{\text{layers}} \times H_{\text{KV}} \times d_k \times S \times P_{\text{bytes}}$$
Where:
* $B$ = Concurrent batch size
* $n_{\text{layers}}$ = Number of transformer layers
* $H_{\text{KV}}$ = Number of Key/Value attention heads (e.g., $8$ in GQA vs. $64$ in MHA)
* $d_k$ = Attention head dimension ($d_{\text{model}} / H_Q$)
* $S$ = Active context length (prompt tokens + generated tokens)
* $P_{\text{bytes}}$ = Precision format bytes

### 3. Activation Memory & Workspace ($M_{\text{activations}} + M_{\text{system}}$)
Temporary tensors allocated during the forward pass (e.g., intermediate matrix multiplication outputs, softmax scores, layer norm buffers). In optimized inference engines utilizing fused CUDA kernels, activation memory during decode is minimal ($O(B \cdot d_{\text{model}})$), but during the **Prefill Phase** on large batch sizes with long sequences, activation tensors can surge to several gigabytes.

